body:
### Version

1.64.0 (also 1.65.0-alpha-2026-10-09). 1.63.0 and earlier are not affected.

### Steps to reproduce

A credentialed `fetch` that carries a non-safelisted request header (so it is preflighted) and is redirected cross-origin fails as soon as **any** route is registered on the context. The route can be one that matches nothing.

Save as `repro.cjs` and run `node repro.cjs` with `playwright-core@1.64.0` installed:

```js
const http = require('http');
const { chromium } = require('playwright-core');

function cors(req, res) {
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', req.headers.origin || '*');
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', 'true');
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'x-app-version');
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET');
}
const page = http.createServer((req, res) => { res.setHeader('content-type', 'text/html'); res.end('<html><body>hi</body></html>'); });
const api = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  cors(req, res);
  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') { res.statusCode = 204; return res.end(); }
  res.statusCode = 302; res.setHeader('Location', 'http://127.0.0.1:4802/file'); res.end();
});
const files = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  cors(req, res);
  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') { res.statusCode = 204; return res.end(); }
  res.end(Buffer.alloc(16464, 7));
});

(async () => {
  await Promise.all([[page, 4800], [api, 4801], [files, 4802]].map(([s, p]) => new Promise(r => s.listen(p, r))));
  const browser = await chromium.launch();
  for (const withRoute of [false, true]) {
    const context = await browser.newContext();
    if (withRoute) await context.route(/^https?:\/\/never\.example\//, r => r.continue());
    const p = await context.newPage();
    await p.goto('http://localhost:4800/');
    const result = await p.evaluate(async () => {
      try {
        const r = await fetch('http://localhost:4801/dl', { credentials: 'include', headers: { 'X-App-Version': '1.2.3' } });
        return `${r.status} ${(await r.arrayBuffer()).byteLength}`;
      } catch (e) { return `ERR ${e}`; }
    });
    console.log(withRoute ? 'withRoute' : 'noRoute  ', result);
    await context.close();
  }
  await browser.close();
  process.exit(0);
})();
```

### Expected behavior

Both runs print `200 16464`. That is what 1.61.1, 1.62.1 and 1.63.0 print.

### Actual behavior

```
noRoute   200 16464
withRoute ERR TypeError: Failed to fetch
```

The redirected request ends with `net::ERR_ABORTED` and `canceled: true`, and it never reaches the target server. There is no CORS error and no `blockedReason`.

### Additional context

**The cause is the Chromium build, not playwright-core.** Swapping browser binaries between versions shows this:

| playwright-core | browser | noRoute | withRoute |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1.61.1 | own (149.0.7827.55) | 200 | 200 |
| 1.63.0 | own (153.0.8010.12) | 200 | 200 |
| 1.64.0 | own (156.0.8078.4) | 200 | **fail** |
| 1.64.0 | 1.61.1's 149 binary | 200 | 200 |
| 1.61.1 | 1.64.0's 156 binary | 200 | **fail** |
| 1.64.0 | Chrome for Testing 155.0.8043.0 | 200 | 200 |
| 1.64.0 | Chrome for Testing 155.0.8047.0 → 157.0.8093.0 | 200 | **fail** |

Raw CDP with no Playwright route handling gives the same split. A `Fetch.enable` whose pattern matches nothing is enough to break it.

**The first bad build** is in the 155.0.8043.0..155.0.8047.0 window, which contains crrev [344021ca3866](https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8278433), "OOR-CORS: Avoid CorsURLLoader restart on redirect" (crbug 513518289). It adds `AvoidCorsURLLoaderRestartOnRedirect`, enabled by default.

**Launching with `--disable-features=AvoidCorsURLLoaderRestartOnRedirect` makes `withRoute` pass on 156.** One possible fix follows the precedent of `BlockOriginHeaderModificationOnRedirect`: add `AvoidCorsURLLoaderRestartOnRedirect` to the disabled features in `chromiumSwitches.ts` until Chromium fixes the interaction with DevTools Fetch interception. As things stand, a user cannot add that flag without restating Playwright's whole `--disable-features` list, because the last switch wins.

**Why this matters:** any app that downloads files through a redirect to object storage with a custom header breaks under test as soon as one `route()` is registered. A common example is an API returning a 302 to a presigned S3 or CloudFront URL.
